WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.529
I am going to turn now to feedback from Board
committees and subcommittees and invite Victor

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:07.680
to introduce it, please.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.680
Feedback from Board Committees and Sub-committees
Victor Adebowale

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.680
Non-Executive Director
CCG Authorisation Committee

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:17.900
The CCG Authorisation Committee has done a
cracking job in getting 211 CCGs authorised.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:25.310
Can I thank Naquib and Ciarán for their support
on this; I think it is true to say we could

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.210
not have done it without them, but in particular
Barbara's team who have just played a blinder.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:34.520
Getting this done in the time available and
the resources available was no mean feat and

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.160
I think they should be congratulated.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.530
We have now moved into the next phase, which
is just as important, which is assuring that

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:49.880
those CCGs that have conditions upon them
meet those conditions and can be fully authorised.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.660
We need to assure ourselves that that is the
case.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.690
We also need to assure ourselves that the
development programme that has been put in

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:02.660
place to improve and support CCGs is delivered,
and is delivered really well.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:08.810
It is my view that we also need to ensure
that we have consistence excellence of leadership

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:14.810
across the CCG network; I do not see why someone
in area X should have any worse leadership

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.020
than area Y, so we need to make sure that
delivers.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.860
Of course the Board, like everyone else, will
be interested in the outcomes of CCGs as they

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.789
operate a new and complex commissioning landscape.

00:01:26.789 --> 00:01:35.210
I am looking forward to working with Rosamond
on that, on delivering all that and reporting

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.649
to the Board as we go forward.

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:38.689
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:44.039
That is something we will want to keep a very
close eye on, because of course, the success

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:49.890
of the CCGs is our success and we wish to
work in a very supportive way and closely

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.109
with them.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:54.249
Any questions for Victor?

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:58.299
If not may I invite Moira to give us an oral
report on the CSU committee?

00:01:58.299 --> 00:01:59.299
Moira Gibb
Non-Executive Director

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:00.299
CSU Committee
Thank you, Chair.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:03.630
The CSU committee met on 22nd April; the minutes
are not available now but will obviously be

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.350
available for the next Board meeting.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:14.310
I will just briefly touch on four points,
if I may.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:20.950
The committee received a report on check point
four of the assurance process on CSUs which

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:26.349
said that all of them were financially viable
in '13, '14 but there were development and

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:28.390
support needs, which would not be surprising
in such new organisations.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.810
Some of them were significant but, nevertheless,
there were plans to meet those.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:37.200
We also heard about the CSS market strategy
and supported the idea of the launch in June

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.019
at the commissioning show, I think it is called.

00:02:43.019 --> 00:02:51.110
Supporting that there was a development of
an app which will help CCGs learn about what

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:59.069
commissioning support is available in a very
user friendly way, I am told.

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:14.900
It will be very up-to-date because it is a
complex financial framework that CSUs have

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:23.200
to work in and the difficulties that presents,
but efforts are being made to try to resolve

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.390
that.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:34.950
Also again, the impact on CSUs of the system-wide
problem of patient information flows and the

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:43.909
new structures that we have and the difficulties;
it is not for CSU people, as it were, to resolve

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:47.250
but it does have an impact on CSUs.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:53.129
Ros will answer any questions that anyone
has on that report.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:57.519
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, and thank you for
volunteering, Ros!

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:02.340
I think you may be spared, but thank you,
Moira, very much for the report, and Ros,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.599
for your work on it.

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:10.310
We turn now to item ten on the agenda, which
is an oral update on the accounts of the Commissioning

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.310
Board.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.310
NHS Commissioning Board Accounts
Paul Baumann

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.310
Chief Financial Officer
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:18.680
This is just a very quick progress update;
last time we met we took in private session

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.380
the accounts for the first half year.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.730
Remember, this is a strange year because we
had the first half year as the Commissioning

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:28.980
Board Authority and we have to lay a set of
accounts for that.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.300
The second half of the year was in the guise
of the Commissioning Board proper, if I can

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.220
put it that way, and we have a second set
of accounts to do -- twice as much fun for

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.710
accountants.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:44.060
The first set of accounts we took last time,
those are just waiting there in the queue

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:48.930
to be laid before parliament and then they
will be published by us on the website, but

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.009
those, as you recall, were problem-free.

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:58.600
The second six month accounts were completed
in draft according to the national timetable

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.759
for that, on 22nd April.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:06.440
They are with the national audit office, which
of course is our external auditor, for checking.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:12.400
They start their work on Tuesday, which will
be interesting for us all I am sure.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.680
That comes back then in its post-audit form
to the audit committee in June in time for

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.780
that then to be published thereafter.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:28.210
Given the timings of the meetings that we
have it will be extremely helpful if the Board

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.230
could empower the audit committee to sign
off on its behalf, those accounts when it

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:38.050
meets in June, and then for you, Malcolm and
David, to be able to do the final signing

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.050
off thereafter.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:44.340
Of course, when we then meet as a Board for
the next time in July, we will bring the accounts

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.870
at that point.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.340
If we do not have that authority we cannot
pass them on according to the timetables we

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.890
are required to work.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:55.874
Members might be interested to know what the
numbers say, as well as the process.

00:05:55.874 --> 00:06:01.920
You will recall that we had for the whole
of last year, across the two six-month periods

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:07.780
that I talked about, just over £80 million
of resources just to fund, effectively, the

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:17.220
central team that [break in audio] to the
board.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.490
The likelihood of an under spend against that
£80 million was always quite high for the

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:26.419
very reason that we talked about today and
earlier, which is that the recruitment of

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:30.080
the team has taken longer than we anticipated.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:37.759
In the accounts we have submitted we have
spent £61.6 million against that control

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:42.040
total, as it were, of £80 million, so the
Board can be assured that we have stayed well

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.680
within our financial control total, and we
have delivered the programme that has been

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.300
described this morning with less resource
than we had originally been allocated.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.669
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:59.889
Of course, the volume of our expenditure is
going to go up exceptionally.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:01.729
Paul Baumann: Just a little.

00:07:01.729 --> 00:07:04.920
Malcolm Grant: Just a little; and you will,
I know, be bringing regular financial reporting

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.610
to the Board starting in July.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:15.409
Any questions for Paul; in which case may
I ask you to endorse his proposal that the

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:22.039
accounts, when they come back, will go to
the audit committee for approval, and then

00:07:22.039 --> 00:07:25.430
to me and to David for final sign-off?

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:26.430
Okay?

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.100
Thank you very much.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.020
That brings us, I think, to the end of the
formal business.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.889
I have no other business.

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:43.520
In which case I think I need to say formally
that the Board will now meet as a private

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.650
meeting with the items on the agenda that
I mentioned before, but otherwise this meeting

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:52.430
is closed and can I again thank members of
the public for joining us.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:53.430
Your presence is much appreciated.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:53.740
Thank you.

